nd PackageOptions objects with conditional logic to handle nullable fields like fragile status, insured value, and handling type.\n\nNow I'm realizing the quality check about keeping methods straightforward conflicts with what the test setup actually needs—those conditional setters are necessary because some parameters are optional, not a design problem. I think the right call is to keep the table structure with individual columns for each optional field and accept that the test method will have some conditional logic in the setup phase. I'm weighing whether to add traceability columns like billable weight to make the logic clearer, but since the user asked for a straightforward conversion of the existing test rather than a redesign, I'll keep it simple and stick closer to the original structure.\n\nFor the `false` values in the fragile column, I can make the table cleaner by treating blank cells as `false` (or `null`) and only explicitly marking `true` where fragile items appear. This matches how TableTest handles nullable booleans and reduces visual clutter compared to spelling out `false` everywhere.\n\nGenerating markdown table... Generating shipping rate table... I'm noticing the test cases show that the same EU express rate of 12.00 applies across different carriers—DHL, UPS, and FEDEX—which means the pricing should be carrier-agnostic for this tier. I can consolidate these four rows into a single row using a value set for the carrier field, since all three carriers (DHL, UPS, FEDEX) produce the same 12.00 result. The value set semantics check out—every value in the set produces identical expected results, so this consolidation is valid and reduces redundancy in the test table. I'm realizing the quality check is specifically about the test method itself, not the setup logic, so I can use conditional statements in the helper method for creating PackageOptions. For the boolean and nullable fields, I can use direct assignments like `opts.setFragile(Boolean.TRUE.equals(fragile))` to avoid if statements where possible, though I'll need to handle the insured value case more carefully.\n\nNow I need to check the dependency setup reference to get the correct coordinates for the TableTest framework.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10398,"cache_read_input_tokens":27770,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":10398,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":13,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"57a4bbfc-014c-4b87-ac7e-02b3e3ee3201","uuid":"e1c72fb9-088f-4d55-bb34-d314ca71cf2d"}
