{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TswDDaAiiLPc9Kq3hHR2aZ","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Value Sets for Multiple Input Examples for Same Expectation\n     2→\n     3→Use `{...}` to expand a single row into multiple test cases. Expansion happens when the parameter type is NOT `Set<?>`. If the parameter type is `Set<?>`, the value is parsed as a set literal instead.\n     4→\n     5→```java\n     6→@TableTest(\"\"\"\n     7→    Scenario                    | Example Years      | Is Leap Year?\n     8→    Not divisible by 4          | {2001, 2002, 2003} | false\n     9→    Divisible by 4              | {2004, 2008, 2012} | true\n    10→    Divisible by 100 not by 400 | {2100, 2200, 2300} | false\n    11→    Divisible by 400            | {2000, 2400, 2800} | true\n    12→    \"\"\")\n    13→void testLeapYears(Year year, boolean isLeapYear) {\n    14→    assertEquals(isLeapYear, year.isLeap());\n    15→}\n    16→```\n    17→\n    18→## Cartesian Product\n    19→\n    20→Multiple sets in the same row create a cartesian product:\n    21→\n    22→```java\n    23→@TableTest(\"\"\"\n    24→    Scenario | a      | b      | Max Sum?\n    25→    Combined | {1, 2} | {3, 4} | 6\n    26→    \"\"\")\n    27→void testCartesianProduct(int a, int b, int maxSum) {\n    28→    assertTrue(a + b <= maxSum);\n    29→}\n    30→```\n    31→\n    32→This generates 4 test cases: (1,3), (1,4), (2,3), (2,4).\n    33→\n    34→## \"Doesn't Matter\" Pattern\n    35→\n    36→Use value sets when a flag is irrelevant for certain scenarios:\n    37→\n    38→```java\n    39→@TableTest(\"\"\"\n    40→    Scenario    | Master | Fallback      | Expected?\n    41→    Normal flow | true   | {true, false} | success\n    42→    Error path  | true   | true          | fallback\n    43→    \"\"\")\n    44→void handles_errors(boolean master, boolean fallback, String expected) { ... }\n    45→```\n    46→\n    47→Row 1: Fallback flag doesn't matter when there's no error, so test both values.\n    48→Row 2: Fallback flag is critical for error handling, so specify exact value.\n    49→\n    50→This documents in the table itself which flags matter for which scenarios. When a column has `{true, false}` for a row, it signals \"this input is irrelevant to the output for this scenario\".\n    51→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"57a4bbfc-014c-4b87-ac7e-02b3e3ee3201","uuid":"48b48e01-06e3-48e4-9c09-297a343817df","timestamp":"2026-04-06T17:23:56.614Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/var/folders/fz/zxx_qbvj49gbql83cc5l47pr0000gn/T/eval-skill-1775493393027/skills/tabletest/references/value-sets.md","content":"# Value Sets for Multiple Input Examples for Same Expectation\n\nUse `{...}` to expand a single row into multiple test cases. Expansion happens when the parameter type is NOT `Set<?>`. If the parameter type is `Set<?>`, the value is parsed as a set literal instead.\n\n```java\n@TableTest(\"\"\"\n    Scenario                    | Example Years      | Is Leap Year?\n    Not divisible by 4          | {2001, 2002, 2003} | false\n    Divisible by 4              | {2004, 2008, 2012} | true\n    Divisible by 100 not by 400 | {2100, 2200, 2300} | false\n    Divisible by 400            | {2000, 2400, 2800} | true\n    \"\"\")\nvoid testLeapYears(Year year, boolean isLeapYear) {\n    assertEquals(isLeapYear, year.isLeap());\n}\n```\n\n## Cartesian Product\n\nMultiple sets in the same row create a cartesian product:\n\n```java\n@TableTest(\"\"\"\n    Scenario | a      | b      | Max Sum?\n    Combined | {1, 2} | {3, 4} | 6\n    \"\"\")\nvoid testCartesianProduct(int a, int b, int maxSum) {\n    assertTrue(a + b <= maxSum);\n}\n```\n\nThis generates 4 test cases: (1,3), (1,4), (2,3), (2,4).\n\n## \"Doesn't Matter\" Pattern\n\nUse value sets when a flag is irrelevant for certain scenarios:\n\n```java\n@TableTest(\"\"\"\n    Scenario    | Master | Fallback      | Expected?\n    Normal flow | true   | {true, false} | success\n    Error path  | true   | true          | fallback\n    \"\"\")\nvoid handles_errors(boolean master, boolean fallback, String expected) { ... }\n```\n\nRow 1: Fallback flag doesn't matter when there's no error, so test both values.\nRow 2: Fallback flag is critical for error handling, so specify exact value.\n\nThis documents in the table itself which flags matter for which scenarios. When a column has `{true, false}` for a row, it signals \"this input is irrelevant to the output for this scenario\".\n","numLines":51,"startLine":1,"totalLines":51}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Sd7GbpnLPNz7okwegh3XKp","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Dependency and Import Setup\n     2→\n     3→Prefer the version already used in the project. If adding a new dependency, check Maven Central and replace `VERSION`.\n     4→\n     5→## Maven\n     6→\n     7→```xml\n     8→<dependency>\n     9→    <groupId>org.tabletest</groupId>\n    10→    <artifactId>tabletest-junit</artifactId>\n    11→    <version>VERSION</version>\n    12→    <scope>test</scope>\n    13→</dependency>\n    14→```\n    15→\n    16→## Gradle\n    17→\n    18→```groovy\n    19→testImplementation \"org.tabletest:tabletest-junit:VERSION\"\n    20→```\n    21→\n    22→## Imports\n    23→\n    24→```java\n    25→import org.tabletest.junit.TableTest;\n    26→```\n    27→\n    28→Import `@Scenario` only when binding the scenario column:\n    29→```java\n    30→import org.tabletest.junit.Scenario;\n    31→```\n    32→\n    33→Import `@TypeConverter` for custom converter methods:\n    34→```java\n    35→import org.tabletest.junit.TypeConverter;\n    36→```\n    37→\n    38→Import `@TypeConverterSources` for shared converter sources:\n    39→```java\n    40→import org.tabletest.junit.TypeConverterSources;\n    41→```\n    42→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"57a4bbfc-014c-4b87-ac7e-02b3e3ee3201","uuid":"499db998-c81e-42b9-a53b-b55efad54d4a","timestamp":"2026-04-06T17:23:56.616Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/var/folders/fz/zxx_qbvj49gbql83cc5l47pr0000gn/T/eval-skill-1775493393027/skills/tabletest/references/dependency-setup.md","content":"# Dependency and Import Setup\n\nPrefer the version already used in the project. If adding a new dependency, check Maven Central and replace `VERSION`.\n\n## Maven\n\n```xml\n<dependency>\n    <groupId>org.tabletest</groupId>\n    <artifactId>tabletest-junit</artifactId>\n    <version>VERSION</version>\n    <scope>test</scope>\n</dependency>\n```\n\n## Gradle\n\n```groovy\ntestImplementation \"org.tabletest:tabletest-junit:VERSION\"\n```\n\n## Imports\n\n```java\nimport org.tabletest.junit.TableTest;\n```\n\nImport `@Scenario` only when binding the scenario column:\n```java\nimport org.tabletest.junit.Scenario;\n```\n\nImport `@TypeConverter` for custom converter methods:\n```java\nimport org.tabletest.junit.TypeConverter;\n```\n\nImport `@TypeConverterSources` for shared converter sources:\n```java\nimport org.tabletest.junit.TypeConverterSources;\n```\n","numLines":42,"startLine":1,"totalLines":42}}}

